WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:07.110
So once again, we've got one of these stories
where a Democrat says, I've been in touch

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:13.090
privately with Republicans who might turn
on Donald Trump and actually convict him if

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:18.260
there were to be an impeachment trial, uh,
where the Senate would vote.

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:24.750
This is democratic Senator from Connecticut,
Chris Murphy, he called it a number of Republicans

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:26.590
that he could count on one hand.

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:32.279
So presumably not more than five Republicans
who he says have told him privately they would

00:00:32.279 --> 00:00:37.600
consider voting to convict Donald Trump in
an impeachment trial and uh, remove him from

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:38.600
office.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:39.859
So let's be responsible.

00:00:39.859 --> 00:00:41.239
Let's evaluate this carefully.

00:00:41.239 --> 00:00:45.489
I must have gotten a dozen emails about this
over the weekend because remember that there

00:00:45.489 --> 00:00:49.920
were claims similar to this that ended up
not coming true between Donald Trump's election

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:50.989
and inauguration.

00:00:50.989 --> 00:00:56.579
At the time there were assertions made, there
were electors who were going to defect and

00:00:56.579 --> 00:01:00.440
deny Donald Trump the presidency altogether.

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:01.979
Obviously didn't happen.

00:01:01.979 --> 00:01:04.170
Many of us have forgotten about that already.

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:09.340
So the question is, are there really five
Republican senators that might consider doing

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:10.340
this?

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:14.810
One possibility is there's no way to know
because they probably are considering this.

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:20.299
So secretly that they've never gone public
about their concerns about Donald Trump or

00:01:20.299 --> 00:01:22.250
their, their complete and total sleepers.

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:24.340
We couldn't possibly know who they are.

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:28.090
If that's the case, it could be any of the
Republican members of the Senate.

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:29.789
What's the point of even speculating.

00:01:29.789 --> 00:01:35.070
But let's for imagine, let's imagine for a
moment that the most likely candidates to

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:40.499
defect are people who have shown some public
departure from supporting Donald Trump.

00:01:40.499 --> 00:01:43.159
So that then we can assemble a list of possibilities.

00:01:43.159 --> 00:01:45.990
And then the list is pretty obvious, at least
at the beginning.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:48.030
One is Susan Collins from Maine.

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:51.539
On the one hand, Susan Collins has spoken
out against Donald Trump.

00:01:51.539 --> 00:01:56.710
In some cases she's been one of the senator's
biggest Republican critics of Donald Trump.

00:01:56.710 --> 00:02:00.609
But remember at the same time, in the end,
she often sides with Trump.

00:02:00.609 --> 00:02:06.179
A great is with the Brett Kavanaugh nomination
where there were people on the left saying,

00:02:06.179 --> 00:02:11.440
Susan Collins is going to prevent the sky,
uh, from, uh, being, uh, confirmed to the

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:12.440
Supreme court.

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:14.510
And in the end she went along with it.

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:18.860
But Susan Collins is one possibility, another
possibility of Republican Senator who might

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:20.060
turn on Trump.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:21.060
It's Mitt Romney.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:24.290
Romney has been one of the biggest, uh, public
Republican critics of Trump.

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:26.430
They've gone back and forth publicly about
their friends.

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:27.430
They're not friends.

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:28.430
They like each other.

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:29.430
They don't like each other.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:34.080
Romney wouldn't be a shock if he voted to
convict, but I also think that much like with

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:39.200
Susan Collins, even if they don't like Trump,
they end up being loyal to the Republican

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.519
party and if the party remains loyal to Trump,
they ultimately do as well.

00:02:43.519 --> 00:02:49.300
Any time that there's these conversations
about more reasonable independent thinking

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:52.819
Republicans, we hear the name of Alaska Senator
Lisa Murkowski.

00:02:52.819 --> 00:02:54.000
That's a possibility.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:59.530
She's been notably not commenting on the impeachment
hearing so far, but it's just speculation

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:04.849
at this point because her name always seems
to end up on these lists and then beyond that

00:03:04.849 --> 00:03:06.640
it's really a lot of guessing.

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:12.799
There's Cory Gardner from Colorado who's name
is thrown around Martha McSally and Arizona

00:03:12.799 --> 00:03:14.220
is a possibility.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:20.610
Thom Tillis in North Carolina, maybe David
Purdue in Georgia, but it really is just speculation.

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:23.620
And in the end, these are political calculations.

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:29.060
If there is truly a Senator that believes
they are better off politically voting to

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:34.500
convict the not, you might see it, but we
should be super careful about this because

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:39.530
this is one of those things where even if
you get every democratic vote in the Senate,

00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:45.200
which is, it's 45 and then you've got Bernie
and Angus King who are independent but tend

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:50.960
to caucus with Democrats, that gets you 47
and imagine that you get every one of the

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:52.359
Republicans I just mentioned, right?

00:03:52.359 --> 00:03:57.939
Imagine you get all seven call-ins, Romney,
Murkowski, Gardner, Mick, Sally Tillison,

00:03:57.939 --> 00:04:03.689
Purdue, that's 54 you still need 67 to convict
on impeachment in the Senate.

00:04:03.689 --> 00:04:10.120
So in the most optimistic scenario where every
Democrat plus the two independence plus these

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:16.630
seven very speculative Republicans all vote
to convict, you're still short by 13 now the

00:04:16.630 --> 00:04:21.580
argument would be if there are seven Republicans
already considering it and we don't know that

00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:27.610
there are and all seven were to do it, then
it might provide cover for other Republicans

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:28.610
to jump in.

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:31.400
And then before you know it, you've got an
impeachment conviction with two thirds of

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:32.450
the Senate.

00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:37.620
I don't see that as happening primarily because
I don't see an impeachment conviction vote

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:42.350
being politically advantageous to that many
Republicans, unless public opinion drastically

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:43.350
changes.

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:48.960
62% of Trumpists said, no matter what Trump
does, no matter what happens, no matter what

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:53.220
he says, I'm not going to reconsider my support
for Donald Trump.

00:04:53.220 --> 00:05:00.240
If that's the case, you might be able to find
a few Republicans who are in districts where,

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:06.730
okay, maybe Republicans there want their Republican
Senator to vote to convict on impeachment.

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:11.900
You're not going to get to 67 and that's not
going to translate to remove removing Donald

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:12.900
Trump via impeachment.

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:14.150
I want to be wrong.

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:15.150
We can hope.

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:19.450
Chris Murphy statement is very interesting,
but when you really analyze it, I'm not seeing

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:22.220
a path to this as a viable reality.

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:26.640
I would love to be wrong and I would love
to hear from you about how I'm wrong or at

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:28.280
least potentially wrong.

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:29.280
Let me know.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:30.280
Leave a reply.

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:30.580
If you're watching on YouTube, send me a tweet
at Dee Pacman.

